@block introduction | start | - | Complete and reversible row operations
@prose Learn to read an augmented matrix as two equations in real x and y, apply a row replacement to every entry, reverse it without losing information, and check a solution in both original equations.
@endblock
@block definition | terms | 1.1 | Columns and complete rows
@prose In [a,b|c], a multiplies x, b multiplies y, and c is the right-hand constant. The vertical bar separates the constant column; it is not division. A solution is an ordered pair (x,y) that satisfies both original equations.
@endblock
@block proposition | rule | 1.2 | Replace every entry in a row
@prose R2<-R2+kR1 means use the old rows, add k copies of row 1 to every entry of row 2, and then replace row 2. The x coefficient, y coefficient, and constant all change together. The retained row stays unchanged.
@display row_rule
R'_2=R_2+kR_1,\qquad R_2=R'_2-kR_1
@endblock
@block proposition | condition | 1.3 | Reversibility and a stated goal
@prose The second expression is the inverse of the first, so a pair satisfying the old two equations satisfies the new two equations and conversely. Scaling is reversible only by a nonzero number. A row operation may preserve common solutions while missing an explicitly requested elimination goal.
@endblock
@block example | worked | 1.4 | A separate complete example
@prose Start with the system x+2y=6 and 3x-y=5, represented by the displayed augmented matrix.
@display worked_given
\left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1&2&6\\3&-1&5\end{array}\right]
@help hint
@prose Replace row 2 by row 2 minus three copies of row 1. Calculate all three entries, then solve the resulting one-variable row exactly.
@help answer
@display
(x,y)=\left(\frac{16}{7},\frac{13}{7}\right)
@help solution
@prose The first replacement gives [3-3(1),-1-3(2)|5-3(6)]=[0,-7|-13]. Thus -7y=-13 and y=13/7. Substitute into the original first equation: x+2(13/7)=6, so x=16/7. Check both original equations: 16/7+26/7=6 and 3(16/7)-13/7=5.
@display worked_step
R_2\leftarrow R_2-3R_1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1&2&6\\0&-7&-13\end{array}\right]
@endblock
@block example | errors | 1.5 | Diagnose the first failure
@prose If a student changes the coefficient columns but leaves a constant untouched, the first incomplete line changes the system. Later arithmetic may correctly follow that bad line. Find the earliest invalid operation before repairing the numeric consequence, and then check the repaired pair in both original equations.
@endblock
@block exercise | practice | 1.6 | Three deliberate sets
@prose The teaching set introduces notation and a direct cancellation. Practice changes signs and row coefficients. The fresh check includes elimination with a fractional multiple of a row whose x coefficient is not 1, then asks for a new pair without a supplied method. Across all sets, the invariant is x,y,constant order and whole-row reversible arithmetic.
@endblock
@block summary | summary | 1.7 | Check the original system
@prose Name the destination row, calculate from the old rows, include the constant column, and use the opposite multiple to explain reversal. Finish by substituting the same ordered pair into both original equations. Singular, inconsistent, free-variable, larger, and written-work systems are outside this bounded family.
@endblock
